<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piero della Francesca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1415–<a href="/years/1492">1492</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Piero di Benedetto de' Franceschi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piero della Francesca was an Italian painter, mathematician and geometer of the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, born in Borgo Santo Sepolcro around 1415. His painting is marked by serene humanism, geometric forms and perspective, and his best-known work is the fresco cycle The History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> in the Basilica of San Francesco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>. He worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> and Rimini and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-da-montefeltro">Federico da Montefeltro</a> at Urbino, where he painted the Flagellation and the double portrait of Federico and Battista Sforza. His treatises on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abacus">abacus</a>, the five regular solids and perspective were later absorbed into Luca Pacioli's writing. His sight failed from about 1480, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sansepolcro">Sansepolcro</a> in 1492.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piero-della-francesca.jpg" alt="Piero della Francesca" width="170" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Resurrection_detail.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Piero was born Piero di Benedetto in Borgo Santo Sepolcro, in modern Tuscany. His father, the tradesman Benedetto de' Franceschi, died before his birth, and he was called Piero della Francesca after his mother, Romana di Perino da Monterchi, who was known as &quot;la Francesca&quot; through her marriage into the Franceschi family. He was most probably apprenticed to the local painter Antonio di Giovanni d'Anghiari, with whom payment documents show him working in 1432 and May 1438. In 1439 he and Domenico Veneziano received payment for frescoes in the church of Sant'Egidio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, now lost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Piero returned to his hometown in 1442 and was elected to the City Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sansepolcro">Sansepolcro</a>. In 1451 he painted in Rimini for the condottiero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismondo-pandolfo-malatesta">Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta</a> the fresco of Malatesta before St Sigismund in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tempio-malatestiano">Tempio Malatestiano</a>, and a portrait of Sigismondo. He was called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> in 1452 to replace Bicci di Lorenzo in painting the frescoes of the basilica of San Francesco, finishing in 1464. The History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> draws on medieval sources collected in the Golden Legend of Jacopo da Varazze. At the summons of Pope Nicholas V he also worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where his frescoes in Santa Maria Maggiore and the Vatican Palace survive only in fragments or not at all.</p><p>At Urbino he carried out several commissions for Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-da-montefeltro">Federico da Montefeltro</a>. The double portrait of Federico and his wife Battista Sforza draws on large bronze medals and stucco roundels with their official portraits, and the Montefeltro Altarpiece dates from 1474. In Urbino he met the mathematician Luca Pacioli and probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-battista-alberti">Leon Battista Alberti</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Piero was trained in mathematics in his youth, most likely for mercantile purposes. Three of his treatises survive: the Trattato d'Abaco, De quinque corporibus regularibus on the five regular solids and De prospectiva pingendi on perspective in painting, which cover arithmetic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, geometry and innovative work in solid geometry and perspective. Much of his work was later absorbed into the writing of others, notably Luca Pacioli, whose Divina proportione, illustrated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>, translated his work on solid geometry.</p><p>His sight began to fail by 1480, but he went on writing treatises. He made his will in 1487 and died on 12 October 1492 in his own house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sansepolcro">Sansepolcro</a>, leaving his possessions to his family and the Church. His geometrical clarity and the light in his paintings later inspired Giorgio de Chirico, Felice Casorati and Balthus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piero della Francesca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
